{"response": "7

Singulair is **montelukast**. The LactMed record describes a historical milk-transfer study in which **seven women** provided milk samples after a single maternal exposure. [LactMed—Montelukast](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK501488/)

Informational only; not medical advice. Compiled from public NIH/NLM/FDA data, which may be incomplete or out of date. Patients should talk to a clinician, pharmacist or poison control before starting, stopping or changing any medication."}

{"response": "**6%**

In the historical study, 6% was the estimated average intake for an exclusively breastfed infant, expressed as a maternal weight-adjusted dosage. It is an **exposure estimate**, not a probability of adverse events. LactMed separately reports that relevant published information on breastfed-infant outcomes was not found as of its revision date. Source: [LactMed—Doxycycline](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK500561/).

Informational only; not medical advice. Compiled from public NIH/NLM/FDA data, which may be incomplete or out of date. Patients should talk to a clinician, pharmacist or poison control before starting, stopping or changing any medication."}

{"response": "**1.26; no, this observational association does not prove causation.**

The abstract reports an overall adjusted relative risk of **1.26** (95% CI, 1.04–1.54), distinct from the unadjusted RR of 1.51 and the dose-subgroup estimates. Because this was a population-based observational cohort study, the association cannot by itself establish that hydroxychloroquine caused the malformations. [PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32961123/]

